/-!
# The dimension induction of the zero-separating surface

An `n`-dimensional zero-separating hypersurface is built by induction on dimension. Given an
`(n−1)`-dimensional faithful zero-separating surface, one fixes a constant *attracting direction* `d`
and, from each point of that boundary surface, draws a line segment in direction `d`, continuing it
to the next surface. The segments sweep out a **ruled surface** whose tangent plane everywhere
contains the ruling direction `d`; gluing these ruled patches assembles the full surface.

This module provides the ruled-surface step — its containment, separation, and subtangency-transfer
facts — together with the linear-feasibility threshold that governs when a ruled patch admits a
surface normal.

On a ruled patch crossing several uncertainty regions, a surface normal at a tight boundary point
must be nonzero and orthogonal to every active ruling/attracting direction. Whether such a normal
exists is a homogeneous linear feasibility question with a sharp threshold at `finrank ℝ E`: fewer
than `finrank` active directions always leave a normal; once they span the ambient space, only the
zero vector is orthogonal to all of them. Consequently the **naive** construction — a single patch
aligned simultaneously to several attracting directions — becomes over-determined once the active
directions span, which first occurs in dimension four. A codimension-1 hypersurface still has a
normal at each smooth point. Craciun's construction controls which fan cones constrain that normal
through its faithful blueprint. The refinement module proves admissibility transfer once a fine
cell contained in a coarse cell is supplied; it does not yet construct the subdivision or show that
the resulting surface patches satisfy the normal constraints. That distinction matters in
dimensions where the active directions may span the ambient space.

## The ruled-surface step

* `ruledSet base d L` — the swept set `{ p + t • d | p ∈ base, t ∈ [0, L] }`, the ruled surface
  generated by translating `base` along the ruling direction `d` for parameter length up to `L`.

* `base_subset_ruledSet` — the base sits inside the ruled set at `t = 0`.

* `ruling_mem_ruledSet` — the ruling segment `{ p + t • d | t ∈ [0, L] }` through any base point lies
  in the ruled set: `d` is the tangent (ruling) direction everywhere.

* `ruledSet_subset_compl_ball_of_outward` — **separation transfers.** If `base` already misses the
  open `r`-ball and the ruling direction is *outward* for a separating unit normal `n`
  (`0 ≤ ⟪n, d⟫`, with `base ⊆ {p | r ≤ ⟪n, p⟫}`), then the ruled set still misses the `r`-ball: the
  sweep never moves a point closer to the separating wall than its base point.

## Tangency transfer: subtangency of base + ruling ⇒ subtangency of the ruled surface

The half-plane region `polyRegion faces` is the working form of a zero-separating surface
(`ZeroSeparatingCurve2D.polyRegion`). A face `(n, a)` is **swept-invariant** for the ruling direction
`d` exactly when `0 ≤ ⟪n, d⟫`: translating by `t • d` (`t ≥ 0`) only increases `⟪n, ·⟫`, so it cannot
leave the half-plane.

* `RulingPreservesFace` / `RulingPreservesRegion` — the swept-invariance condition as a `Prop`.

* `ruledSet_subset_polyRegion_of_rulingPreserves` — if the base lies in `polyRegion faces` and the
  ruling direction preserves every face, the entire ruled set lies in `polyRegion faces`: subtangency
  of the base surface plus subtangency of the ruling direction gives subtangency of the swept surface.

* `rulingPreserves_of_inner_nonneg` — the per-face preservation criterion from `0 ≤ ⟪n, d⟫`.

## The over-determination threshold

A valid surface normal `n` on a ruled patch crossing constraint directions `v₀, …, v_{k−1}` must be
nonzero and orthogonal to every `vᵢ`. Whether such `n` exists is governed by whether the `vᵢ` span,
with a sharp threshold at `finrank ℝ E`:

* `exists_orthogonal_normal_of_card_lt_finrank` — if the number of constraint vectors is strictly less
  than `finrank ℝ E`, a nonzero `n` orthogonal to all of them exists. A ruled patch carrying fewer
  active directions than the ambient dimension always has a normal (e.g. two directions in ℝ³).

* `not_exists_orthogonal_normal_of_span_top` — if the constraint vectors *span* `E`, the only vector
  orthogonal to all of them is `0`: no valid surface normal. This is the over-determination of the
  naive single-patch construction, conditional on its active directions spanning.

* `over_determined_in_dim_four` — the concrete `n = 4` instance: four constraint vectors *can* span
  ℝ⁴ (the standard basis does), killing the normal, whereas any three vectors in ℝ⁴ leave a nonzero
  orthogonal normal. The threshold sits exactly at `finrank = 4`; this does not assert that any
  particular construction's active directions span (that count is not established here).

* `inductionStep_of_ruledBuild` — assembles the step from a faithful boundary surface, a ruling
  direction preserving the ambient region, and a *witness* of a compatible swept region. The witness
  carries the decomposition obligation — the part that is over-determined for the naive construction
  in dimension `≥ 4` and that refinement is needed to discharge.

## Scope

This module proves the ruled-set constructions and their containment/separation facts, the
swept-invariance transfer of subtangency from base + ruling to the ruled surface, and the
feasibility/over-determination dichotomy for the surface-normal constraint system, with the explicit
four-dimensional instance. Not constructed here: the explicit decomposition of a neighborhood into
ruled patches each crossing a single uncertainty region (carried as the `hbuild` witness of
`inductionStep_of_ruledBuild`), and the analysis identifying the per-patch attracting directions.

Depends on: `CRNT.Geometry.ZeroSeparatingSurface`,
`CRNT.Geometry.FaithfulCurve2D`, `Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Constructions`,
`Mathlib.Analysis.InnerProductSpace.Projection.Submodule`.
-/

